res of public safety  so i'm not going to answer the  sentencing question i think in a very  direct way  all right i don't think pilate got more  years fixes policing fair enough  i'll come to katie then how do you think  the judge will rule based on the  arguments for a higher sentence  of greater than 150 months man it's a  tough question right and i want to go  around the edges of it myself with this  because i  definitely believe that the aggravating  upper departures are  essentially met in all cases especially  with respect to particular cruelty and  abuse of authority  i believe with the sense of public  outcry and also the fact that he was  guilty in all charges  is going to convince the judge to go a  little bit higher than the 12 and a half  years  agreed that more time doesn't  necessarily solve all these ills  but i think we should expect something a  little higher than the 12 and a half but  not the max  i can see that as well maybe somewhere  around 30 years and be sure to tune in  to the law and crime network this friday  for an hour-long special on the derrick  chauvin trial hosted by anjanet levy  coming up on law and crime daily a  tryout 14 months in the making  the major news for the murder trial of  robert durst ahead  of a coronavirus hiatus plus more  accusations against marilyn manson  why one well-known actress is now suing  the singer in federal court  [Music]  we're back an actress best known for her  role in game of thrones is now suing  marilyn manson  accusing the singer of sexual abuse  court record show esme bianco says  manson engaged in a year-long campaign  of control and abuse against her  according to the complaint manson  attempted to force her to perform sexual  acts  including tying her to a prayer kneeler  beating her with a whip  apparently used by nazis and  electrocuting her manson whose real name  is brian warner  is denying the allegations through his  attorney the lawsuit  filed in california federal court also  names manson's talent manager  as a co-defendant let's bring in civil  rights attorney katie smith and  executive director of partners for  justice emily galvin on monza  emily bianco's allegations are that  manson basically sexually assaulted and  trafficked her  should we be expecting criminal charges  out of this case  that depends on the decision of the l.a  county district attorney and whether  a police report is going to be filed by  anyone in l.a county i think people  forget that the barriers to entry in the  criminal legal system are really  sometimes very simple procedural  barriers i also don't know what the  statute of limitations would be because  it varies based on the charge that the  district attorney would choose so there  may be other legal barriers to a local  case  that being said you know it also depends  on what evidence comes out on the manson  side in federal court  whether there's enough to inspire the  district attorney to move forward or  whether these allegations really are  provably false  all right so we got to see what  allegations come on this federal case  maybe uh katie manson's lawyer is saying  these allegations are provably false  what should we expect next in this case  statute limitations jumps out at me  right away but beyond that provably  false makes  leads me to believe that there's some  evidence that number one these acts were  consensual  or two that they never happened remember  this was a long time ago we're talking  2009 through 2011.  so i expect provably false means we're  going to see some  actual video footage or text messages  something to read about these charges  yeah i would see a consent being a  strong argument here for the defense  by the description of some of the scars  uh if there are scars there then that  would be hard to show  anything but consent but we'll see how  that plays out when we come back  a woman allegedly shot by her  ex-boyfriend and survived takes the  stand against him  the testimony you don't want to miss  next  [Music]  welcome back the long crime network is  set to be the only camera in the  courtroom  when the highly anticipated murder trial  of robert durst resumes later this month  the new york real estate air is accused  of killing his best friend  susan berman in her los angeles home in  december 2000.  durst trial had three days of openings  and 10 witnesses take the stand  before covet 19 postponed the trial on  march 12  2020. the trial judge has repeatedly  denied motions by the defense to declare  a mistrial  over the 14-month long adjournment the  trial could last  up to six months live capital gavel  coverage begins on may 17th  and the latest trial on the law and  crime network is underway terry austin  is here to tell us  more about the state of wisconsin versus  joshua aide  aide a resident of the state was charged  back in august with murdering  his ex-girlfriend's father james  groutnier  aide is also charged with two counts of  attempted murder for shooting his  ex-girlfriend  rebecca burkowski and family friend john  miller police say aide was dating his  girlfriend five years before she called  it quits in march  2020. according to court records text  messages show his  ex wanted him to remove a broken down  car  from her property i was standing next to  john  we were looking over the radiator so  if the radiator is in front of me and  i'm looking at the hood  john was standing to my right and the  next thing i knew  there was josh um at first he just kind  of i just kind of looked at him  and he said hey boo what you doing  and i went to turn i didn't say anything  to him i just went to turn because i was  starting to put tools away i had a 10  millimeter socket in my hand and i had  turned to the left so put that in to the  toolbox  there was a laser pointed in the middle  of my forehead  and i said what the fuck josh  he had fired he tried to fire when that  laser was on the middle of my forehead  and he missed and i was able to move  just enough and um the next thing i knew  as i was  falling um i kept saying i was flying  i really i thought i was i thought i was  gone i just i was  i fell and and the next thing i knew  i was on my side i had woken up and i  had seen  a police officer next to my dad's ford  focus  and my eyes shifted to the right and  that's where i saw my dad laying  i remember saying help  i remember my dad wasn't moving  but i i had also just spoken out for  come too and i was  vomiting and my my ear was ringing  and of course that was rebecca burkowski  the most important witness in this case  she is one of the victims who was shot  in the ear  you heard her testify it was her  ex-boyfriend  who is on trial joshua aide  thanks terry emily and katie are here  now to break down the aid trial  katie i know that we all swoon for a  good opening statement but what did you  think of the defense skipping it in this  case  you're right brian every time i wait for  that opening statement  to soak up what exactly the defense is  going to do and i  like you brian tend to swoon over a very  solid opening statement so the fact that  one was not made  leads me to believe the defense isn't  sure what the prosecution's going to put  that there  there or they don't know what they're  going to put out there it's puzzling  though  in a case of an affirmative defense if  you're going for  a reason of insanity or self-defense you  would want to put that out there in an  opening statement so  we will see what is yet to come emily on  motion papers  the defense kind of put out there that  this is a self-defense case do you see  that or is something missing  well in order to prove self-defense you  need somebody some form of evidence  usually in the form of witness testimony  saying it was self-defense now let's  think about who was there  um two of the people are complainants in  this case one person  is deceased the only person who can  really make it a self-defense case  is mr aide if he 